WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.980
Welcome to This Explainer. Today, we're
tackling something that usually makes

00:03.980 --> 00:08.580
people's eyes just completely glaze over.
Financial accounting, enterprise risk

00:08.580 --> 00:12.620
management, and corporate governance. Now,
I know what you're thinking. Sounds like a

00:12.620 --> 00:17.020
bunch of dry, dusty textbook jargon,
right? Well, what if I told you these

00:17.020 --> 00:21.900
massive corporate frameworks are actually
just basic survival mechanics? To decode

00:21.900 --> 00:25.380
the whole shebang, we're going to step out
of the boardroom and head straight into

00:25.380 --> 00:29.920
the totally chaotic kitchen of a fictional
pizza shop called The Corner Bistro. All

00:29.920 --> 00:34.720
right, so our story kicks off with what
looks like a massive win. Meet Alex, the

00:34.720 --> 00:38.880
co-owner of The Bistro. It's the end of
the month, and he is absolutely thrilled,

00:39.380 --> 00:43.900
celebrating a whopping 30% profit on
paper. Everything seems perfect, right?

00:44.240 --> 00:49.000
Well, his partner, Sarah, steps in with
some serious skepticism. She takes one

00:49.000 --> 00:53.400
look at the spreadsheet and drops a
massive reality check. She says, a 30%

00:53.400 --> 00:56.820
profit on the books is just an illusion if
you don't know where it actually came

00:56.820 --> 01:01.300
from. See, Sarah knows that just because
the Excel math works out, it doesn't

01:01.300 --> 01:05.500
automatically mean the business is
actually healthy. You can really see the

01:05.500 --> 01:09.300
massive difference here between just
having numerical accuracy and

01:09.300 --> 01:13.680
understanding the actual essence of the
business. Alex's joy? It's completely

01:13.680 --> 01:18.240
driven by surface metrics. But Sarah is
hunting for the absolute truth of origin.

01:18.660 --> 01:23.100
Think about it. Did they make that 30%
profit because a new recipe was a huge

01:23.100 --> 01:26.620
hit? Or did they make it because they
accidentally forgot to pay the cheese

01:26.620 --> 01:30.580
supplier for three weeks? Because one of
those means growth, and the other means a

01:30.580 --> 01:34.260
looming lawsuit, right? So the big
question is, how does an organization

01:34.260 --> 01:39.340
actually see the real operational facts of
what has happened? Well, that brings us to

01:39.340 --> 01:42.540
the very first whiteboard that Alex and
Sarah hang up in the back office of The

01:42.540 --> 01:47.060
Bistro. They label it financial
accounting. Now, in the context of our

01:47.060 --> 01:51.100
story, financial accounting isn't just
about doing the math correctly. It's the

01:51.100 --> 01:55.260
literal process of seeing the real
operational facts and heavily focusing on

01:55.260 --> 01:59.380
verifying that truth of origin behind the
numbers. It's all about figuring out the

01:59.380 --> 02:02.740
difference between a technically correct
number and the actual reality of the

02:02.740 --> 02:06.020
business. You know, you have to physically
walk down into the shop, check the

02:06.020 --> 02:10.200
inventory, and make sure the numbers
actually match what's in the fridge. So

02:10.200 --> 02:13.820
the rule of the first whiteboard is
simple. Good news on the books must

02:13.820 --> 02:18.560
always, always be verified down in the
shop. But hey, tracking the past is really

02:18.560 --> 02:22.940
only half the battle. What about the stuff
happening right now, in the moment? We

02:22.940 --> 02:27.000
absolutely have to anticipate operational
problems before they completely derail our

02:27.000 --> 02:32.280
goals. Imagine it's Friday night, 7.30pm,
and the kitchen chaos is in full swing.

02:32.420 --> 02:36.660
Can the Bistro handle the real-time
pressure? Spoiler alert, they absolutely

02:36.660 --> 02:42.560
cannot. Just watch how fast this
escalates. At 7pm, the order tickets start

02:42.560 --> 02:48.600
piling up. By 7.30, the main line cook,
Xiao Zhao, is completely overwhelmed. He's

02:48.600 --> 02:53.040
drowning in tickets, losing track of bake
times. It's a total mess. And at 8pm,

02:53.460 --> 02:58.460
total unmitigated disaster. The main pizza
oven literally starts smoking. Pizzas are

02:58.460 --> 03:02.400
burning to a crisp, customers are furious,
and the entire kitchen just grinds to a

03:02.400 --> 03:06.160
halt. This right here is exactly what
happens when you only track problems

03:06.160 --> 03:10.660
passively. They saw Xiao Zhao sweating,
sure, but nobody actually did a thing

03:10.660 --> 03:15.760
until the oven was quite literally on
fire. So the absolutely crucial point here

03:15.760 --> 03:20.080
is figuring out how an organization
anticipates these problems before they

03:20.080 --> 03:23.980
burn the kitchen down. How do we make sure
a smoking oven never, ever happens again?

03:24.160 --> 03:27.620
Because we already know that just staring
at past financial records from Whiteboard

03:27.620 --> 03:31.240
One, yeah, that's not going to save us
from a kitchen fire on a Friday night. We

03:31.240 --> 03:35.800
need a reliable way to spot the danger way
before the smoke even appears. And this

03:35.800 --> 03:40.420
highlights a mistake that so many
businesses make every single day. The idea

03:40.420 --> 03:44.960
of passive color coding. It's like
management saying, okay, if we get more

03:44.960 --> 03:49.940
than 15 tickets, the screen turns yellow.
20, it turns red. But guess what? A red

03:49.940 --> 03:54.520
warning label doesn't bake a pizza. What
the corner bistro actually needs are rigid

03:54.520 --> 03:59.020
action boundaries. We're talking about
defining explicit, hard thresholds for

03:59.020 --> 04:03.340
immediate intervention. It means
establishing a rule that says, hey, if we

04:03.340 --> 04:07.440
hit 20 tickets, we automatically stop
taking delivery orders for exactly 15

04:07.440 --> 04:12.200
minutes. And that perfectly tees up our
second whiteboard, enterprise risk

04:12.200 --> 04:17.900
management, or ERM. ERM is all about
setting those pre-warning indicators and

04:17.900 --> 04:22.120
those strict action boundaries so you can
anticipate risks before they absolutely

04:22.120 --> 04:26.880
blow up your kitchen. It's what shifts a
business from being purely reactive to

04:26.880 --> 04:32.020
proactively taking charge. ERM proves that
bad news, like a red flashing light on a

04:32.020 --> 04:36.560
screen, must be met with immediate pre
-planned action. It's essentially the

04:36.560 --> 04:41.120
system that catches the falling knife
before it actually hits the floor. Okay,

04:41.300 --> 04:45.760
so now we have a way to verify the past,
right? And we have a way to spot risks in

04:45.760 --> 04:51.300
the present. But there is still a massive
glaring hole in this whole plan. Think

04:51.300 --> 04:55.600
about it. When that alarm sounds, when the
ticket count hits 20 and the screen starts

04:55.600 --> 05:00.000
flashing red, who actually takes the
action? Because having an alarm is

05:00.000 --> 05:04.580
completely useless if everyone just stands
around staring at it, assuming somebody

05:04.580 --> 05:09.100
else is going to step in. Which brings us
to the final and maybe most critical piece

05:09.100 --> 05:13.280
of the puzzle. To fix this whole bystander
effect, the team has to hang up a third

05:13.280 --> 05:18.000
whiteboard with three really clear steps.
Step one, delegate decision rates. When

05:18.000 --> 05:21.560
the kitchen gets slammed, Zhao Zhou needs
the explicit authority to just say, stop

05:21.560 --> 05:24.200
taking orders, without having to
frantically call the owner for permission.

05:24.200 --> 05:28.460
Step two, establish oversight mechanisms.
Someone needs to be checking to make sure

05:28.460 --> 05:31.000
Zhao Zhou isn't just turning off the
orders because he wants to sneak out for

05:31.000 --> 05:35.340
an early break, right? And step three,
ensure the system works continuously, even

05:35.340 --> 05:39.700
when Alex and Sarah aren't actually in the
building. This third whiteboard, that

05:39.700 --> 05:44.000
represents governance. Governance is the
framework that clarifies those decision

05:44.000 --> 05:48.360
rights, the oversight powers, and the
exact mechanisms needed to make sure

05:48.360 --> 05:52.700
operational systems run effectively. If
financial accounting is the reality check

05:52.700 --> 05:57.480
and ERM is the alarm system, then
governance is the chain of command. It

05:57.480 --> 06:01.180
guarantees that someone is always
unquestionably responsible for handling

06:01.180 --> 06:04.820
the bad news. It basically makes sure
those other two whiteboards aren't just

06:04.820 --> 06:08.720
decorative wall art. All right, let's step
out of the pizza shop for a second.

06:08.940 --> 06:12.740
Because the reality is, these three
whiteboards aren't just for small,

06:12.920 --> 06:17.740
independent businesses. Let's zoom out and
take these exact same concepts straight

06:17.740 --> 06:22.040
into the global boardroom. The scale
obviously changes, but the core mechanics

06:22.040 --> 06:26.660
of survival, they remain completely
identical. The exact dynamics playing out

06:26.660 --> 06:31.660
between Alex, Sarah, and Zhao Zhao are the
foundational principles of massive,

06:31.900 --> 06:35.840
complex corporate structures. Think about
seeking the truth of origin in the pizza

06:35.840 --> 06:39.840
shop. Well, that's exactly what global
financial information systems do to make

06:39.840 --> 06:44.080
sure audits actually match corporate
reality. And setting action boundaries to

06:44.080 --> 06:47.980
prevent a smoking oven? That is the
beating heart of the Cozo enterprise risk

06:47.980 --> 06:52.180
management and NFR frameworks. These are
the global standards designed to flag

06:52.180 --> 06:56.440
risks way before they can crash a
company's stock price. And delegating who

06:56.440 --> 06:59.680
has the power to stop the kitchen line?
That's identical to the governance

06:59.680 --> 07:03.740
structures you'd find in the annual report
of a huge multinational like Royal DSM.

07:04.480 --> 07:09.520
Literally, pages 123 to 146 detail the
exact oversight powers of their managing

07:09.520 --> 07:14.760
board. It really is just Zhao Zhao's
kitchen rules, just scaled way, way up.

07:15.360 --> 07:19.180
Ultimately, whether you're managing a
corner bistro on a chaotic Friday night,

07:19.220 --> 07:22.540
or you're sitting on the board of a
multinational corporation trying to

07:22.540 --> 07:27.320
navigate global supply chain risks, there
is one core motto you absolutely must live

07:27.320 --> 07:32.460
by to survive. Good news on books must be
verified in the shop. Bad news on the wall

07:32.460 --> 07:36.640
must be handled. You simply cannot run a
business on assumptions. And you

07:36.640 --> 07:40.580
definitely can't manage risk by just
staring at alarms. You need the truth. You

07:40.580 --> 07:44.300
need the boundaries. And you need the
governance to actually act. If you want to

07:44.300 --> 07:48.540
see how these frameworks look in their raw
corporate form, definitely dive into the

07:48.540 --> 07:53.140
references provided, like the COSO ERM
framework or that Royal DSM annual report,

07:53.300 --> 07:57.540
just to see real corporate governance in
action. Now, as we wrap up today's

07:57.540 --> 08:00.600
explainer, I want to leave you with a
question to think about regarding your own

08:00.600 --> 08:04.480
projects. Do you actually know who has the
authority to shut down the line when the

08:04.480 --> 08:07.860
oven starts smoking? Or are you all just
staring at a red light waiting for someone

08:07.860 --> 08:13.360
else to make a move? Thanks for joining me
and keep questioning the numbers. Thank

08:13.360 --> 08:14.880
you. Thank you.

